# Changelog

## Unreleased

- Update Certbot to v5.8.0 and build Supercronic with Go 1.26.9.
- Upgrade urllib3 to 2.8.0 and remove leftover 2.7 metadata. Remove pip
  from the runtime image because pip 26.2.1 vendors urllib3 2.7.0. The
  weekly scan was failing on CVE-2026-97687 and CVE-2026-97689, and on Go
  stdlib CVE-2026-78667, CVE-2026-78669, and CVE-2026-97031 in the 1.26.8
  build.
- Upgrade Alpine packages at image build so util-linux/libuuid pick up the
  2.41.6 security fixes (CVE-2026-53612, CVE-2026-53613, CVE-2026-53614,
  CVE-2026-76642, CVE-2026-78408, CVE-2026-78410).
- Retry Supercronic module downloads when proxy.golang.org resets.
- Upgrade setuptools and msgpack in the runtime. Ignore the two HIGH
  Python advisories Trivy still attributes to leftover Certbot-base
  package metadata (CVE-2025-47273, GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964).
- Update Go to 1.26.6 and cryptography to 50.0.0 to resolve the fixable high
  severity vulnerabilities reported by the scheduled image scan.

## 2026.08.01

- Remove the unused `uv` and `uvx` tooling inherited from the Certbot base
  image so vulnerable Rust dependencies are not shipped in the runtime image.

## 2026.07.16

- Build Supercronic 0.2.47 with Go 1.26.5 to include current Go security fixes.
- Replace persistent `doas` access with a root entrypoint that drops permanently
  to the configured `certbot` UID and GID.
- Rewrite the crontab on startup so container restarts cannot duplicate jobs.
- Validate required configuration, the credentials mount, and the cron schedule
  before starting the scheduler.
- Preserve the original `VERSION`, `CERTBOT_VERSION`, `USER_UID`, and
  `USER_GID` build argument interface.
- Allow one published image to use stack-specific runtime UIDs and GIDs.
- Publish tested multi-platform images to GitHub Container Registry.
- Add automated lint, build, smoke-test, and vulnerability-scan coverage.
- Separate the container release version from the upstream
  `certbot-dns-ionos` plugin version.
- Publish immutable version, source-SHA, and `latest` tags from release tags.
- Create GitHub releases from the matching changelog entry.

## 2024.1.8

- First release.
